# 8. Performance Audit: How to Make the ERP Faster

The source includes several useful performance improvements:

* Parallel data loading.
* Short-lived Google Sheets read caching.
* Faster login response.
* Background loading of business records.
* Increased API timeouts to accommodate slow service startup.

These improvements should be retained.

But there is an architectural limit.

## The current bottleneck

Google Sheets is not a purpose-built transactional database.

As your records grow, the application may need to read and process increasingly large collections of rows.

That can affect:

* Dashboard loading.
* Sales history.
* Stock calculations.
* Production reports.
* Employee records.
* Daily closing.
* Concurrent transactions.

A ten-second read cache can reduce repeated requests, but it cannot replace a database designed for transactional workloads.

## Recommended performance plan

| Priority | Upgrade                               | Expected benefit                          |
| -------- | ------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- |
| P0       | Reliable persistent storage           | Prevent lost or misleading transactions   |
| P0       | Storage health monitoring             | Detect failures quickly                   |
| P1       | Paginated API responses               | Avoid loading entire histories            |
| P1       | Database indexes                      | Faster searches and record retrieval      |
| P1       | Background report generation          | Keep the interface responsive             |
| P1       | Reduce unnecessary frontend refreshes | Less waiting and fewer requests           |
| P2       | Lazy-load large screens               | Faster initial application load           |
| P2       | Cache suitable read-only reports      | Faster dashboard access                   |
| P2       | Compress and split frontend bundles   | Faster downloads                          |
| P3       | Dedicated analytics layer             | Faster historical reporting as data grows |

### Suggested performance targets

These are proposed engineering targets, not measurements of your current deployment.

* Dashboard: under 2 seconds under normal conditions.
* Common data entry screens: under 2 seconds.
* Save confirmation: under 1.5 seconds under normal conditions.
* Search: under 1 second for common queries.
* Long reports: display progress rather than freezing the interface.
* Storage failure: show an immediate, understandable warning.

Actual targets should be measured on your deployment and adjusted according to real network conditions.

